  "query_preview": "..\n\nWEATHER PATTERN OVERVIEW:\n\nUpper troughing over much of the western CONUS will bring well \nbelow normal temperatures and areas of precipitation through the \nbeginning of the work week. Precipitation will begin to push east \ninto eastern MT around 6 PM this afternoon and continue through \nMonday night. There is decent chance windy conditions may overlap\nwith a dry slot over southeastern zones tonight into tomorrow \nmorning. The 12Z GFS from yesterday and 12Z GFS ensemble from\ntoday continue to show a dry slot setting up over the central part\nof the area tonight, sort in the shape of an upside down U.\nPrairie, Garfield, Western Roosevelt, and McCone counties are the\nmost likely places to see the dry slot, so precipitation amounts \nare below one tenth in that area through Tuesday. For all other\ncounties, the most likely range of total rainfall is one tenth to\naround a half an inch. There is less than one inch of snow\nforecast to accumulate in southwest Phillips county through\nTuesday, and in the far northern parts of the area.  \n\nWhile thunderstorms are unlikely with this system, there is a 10\nto 15 percent chance for thunder tonight. As the low pressure \nmoves in, some CAMs indicate wind gusts exceeding 50 mph in the \nfar southeast tonight.\n\nThe upper trough will continue to remain over the area after\nTuesday, although a bit weaker than this weekend. This will likely\nallow shortwave troughs to sneak through the overall flow,\nbringing some isolated thunderstorm action to the area. The next\nchance for thunderstorms appears to be Wednesday. Who's ready for\na rollercoaster??\n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS:\nMade a deviation to the base forecast in the far southeast part of\nthe area tonight by increasing winds manually based on CAM models.\n\nThere is high (80 percent) confidence in the Lake Wind Advisory \nfor Fort Peck Lake for tonight. \n\nThere is moderate to high (50 to 70 percent) confidence in total\nrainfall amounts through Tuesday.\n\nThere is moderate confidence that any snow that falls in the north\nand far west will remain below one inch of accumulation.\n\nThere is low to moderate confidence in scattered thunderstorms\noccurring on Wednesday afternoon.",
